 \subsection{Safe Reinforcement Learning for Adaptive Safety Layers}
 Safe reinforcement learning is commonly formulated as a constrained Markov decision process (CMDP), where the objective maximizes the expected return while satisfying expected cost constraints \cite{altman1999cmdp}. Surveys highlight recurring themes: constraint satisfaction can be enforced through penalty shaping, Lagrangian relaxation, shielding/safety layers, or model-based reachability, each with different sample efficiency and guarantee trade-offs \cite{garcia2015survey}. For on-policy policy-gradient methods, Lagrangian-based updates remain popular due to simplicity and compatibility with scalable deep RL.
 
-Constrained Policy Optimization (CPO) provides trust region style updates that approximately satisfy constraints during learning \cite{achiam2017cpo}, while PPO offers a clipped surrogate objective that is widely adopted for stable on-policy training \cite{schulman2017ppo}. Practical constrained PPO variants often use Lagrangian relaxation, and PID-style updates have been proposed to improve the responsiveness of the Lagrange multiplier under changing constraint violations \cite{stooke2020pid}. Benchmarking environments such as Safety Gym have helped standardize the empirical evaluation of constraint satisfaction in continuous control \cite{ray2019safetygym}. In this context, Learning Adaptive Safety for Multi-Agent Systems demonstrates that adaptive CBF parameterization can be learned to balance feasibility, safety, and performance in multi-agent settings \cite{berducci2024las}. Building on these ideas, the present work focuses on collaborative autonomous driving and adapts CBF enforcement online while keeping perception and planning frozen, targeting system-level safety outcomes under V2X-enabled interaction \cite{liu2024v2xverse}.
